{"reference":"2 Peter 3","book":"2 Peter","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter answers doubt about the promised coming by recalling creation and the flood, then turns to the coming day of the Lord, cosmic dissolution, and the promised new heavens and new earth. Its closing call is not speculation but steadfast growth in the grace and knowledge of Jesus Christ.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 3:10","2 Peter 3:12","2 Peter 3:13","2 Peter 3:17-18","2 Peter 3:5-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_003_874998744ee702931df53e55"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Remembering and Waiting","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Reminder frames the chapter: the writer says that this second letter is meant to awaken sincere understanding by recalling earlier prophetic and apostolic teaching. The comparison of one day with a thousand years works in both directions before delay is explained as patience, so it relativizes human timing rather than supplying a timetable for prophetic calculation.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 3:1-2","2 Peter 3:8-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_003_b3ac07caef8f8efd9ea708e7"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Delay, Patience, and Judgment","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The mockers’ objection rests on the apparent continuity of ordinary life: if everything seems unchanged, where is the promised coming? The chapter answers by portraying divine patience as aimed at repentance rather than as failure to keep a promise. Its language about all reaching repentance is broad, but readers and traditions differ over what it implies about divine will, human freedom, and final salvation.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 3:1-2","2 Peter 3:15-16","2 Peter 3:16","2 Peter 3:3-4","2 Peter 3:9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_003_edf563d9817da87255306727"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Several details invite caution. The “second letter” may refer to First Peter, a lost letter, or a literary device; the text itself does not decide. The mockers are a rhetorical portrayal, not a recoverable census of skeptics, so questions about delay should not automatically be treated as moral corruption. Likewise, the flood serves the letter’s judgment-and-rescue argument; geological, chronological, and scientific claims require evidence beyond that rhetorical use. The wording of 3:10 also has a major textual difficulty, with readings involving burning, disappearance, discovery, or related ideas; no single translation should carry a total-destruction doctrine by itself.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 3:1","2 Peter 3:10","2 Peter 3:3-4","2 Peter 3:5-7","2 Peter 3:9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_003_b6a396a1ba33ef9aa04b1979"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The term translated “elements” can mean elements or cosmic constituents and has a wider range than a modern scientific term, so it should not be confidently equated with modern physics. The promised new heavens and new earth may be understood as transformation, renewal, or replacement in different readings; the passage’s stated center is that righteousness dwells there. The chapter’s cosmic-fire imagery is joined to holy conduct and new creation, not to indifference toward the world or threatened communities and species.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 3:1-2","2 Peter 3:10","2 Peter 3:10-13","2 Peter 3:12","2 Peter 3:13","2 Peter 3:15-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_003_ed644ba04872a86110c3cc86"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Reading Difficult Texts","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Paul is called a beloved brother, while his letters are acknowledged to contain difficult material. The comparison with “the other scriptures” is important evidence for the reception of Pauline letters, but it does not list a closed New Testament canon or identify every letter then known. The warning about distortion calls for careful interpretation; it does not authorize anti-intellectualism, exclusion of disabled people, educational gatekeeping, or punishment of sincere questions.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 3:15-16","2 Peter 3:16","2 Peter 3:3-9","2 Peter 3:8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_003_c5a884f165f7f843cea35f67"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Literary Parallel","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"Jude 4–18 and 2 Peter 2:1–3:3 are closely related in wording and literary pattern. The relationship is likely literary, but whether one letter used the other, both drew on a shared source, or another mechanism is involved remains disputed. The parallels therefore should not erase the distinct voice of either letter.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 3:1-3"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_003_6fdca5e5352703af4a877f18"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading the Wider Letter Responsibly","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"The wider letter’s polemical language must not be used to justify antisemitism, supersessionism, dehumanizing opponents, misogyny, anti-LGBTQ coercion, nationalism, colonial mission, forced conversion, religious violence, prosperity extraction, or ecological neglect.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 1:1-21","2 Peter 2:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_003_10f009254bfff4d8f4167677"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}